 any noise inside it.\n\nOne detail for robustness: a frame can have a stray same‑colour cell touching its border (in train 1 a colour‑3 frame has an extra cell under one corner), so the component's bounding box may be one larger than the real frame. I therefore test the bbox and the bbox shrunk by one on each side, and accept the largest candidate whose four sides are completely the frame colour.\n\nThe construction stages: first isolate the chosen frame (crop with its border), then strip the border to leave the interior, which is the final output.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.asarray(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    best = None\n    for o in objects(g, bg):\n        if o[\"size\"] < 8:\n            continue\n        c = o[\"colour\"]\n        Y0, X0, Y1, X1 = o[\"box\"]\n        for y0 in (Y0, Y0 + 1):\n            for y1 in (Y1, Y1 - 1):\n                for x0 in (X0, X0 + 1):\n                    for x1 in (X1, X1 - 1):\n                        if y1 - y0 < 2 or x1 - x0 < 2:\n                            continue\n                        if not ((g[y0, x0:x1 + 1] == c).all() and (g[y1, x0:x1 + 1] == c).all()\n                                and (g[y0:y1 + 1, x0] == c).all() and (g[y0:y1 + 1, x1] == c).all()):\n                            continue\n                        area = (y1 - y0 - 1) * (x1 - x0 - 1)\n                        if best is None or area > best[0]:\n                            best = (area, y0, x0, y1, x1)\n    if best is None:\n        return g.copy()\n    _, y0, x0, y1, x1 = best\n    framed = g[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1].copy()\n    emit(framed, \"Select the hollow rectangular frame with the largest interior and cut it out of the noisy grid, border included\")\n    inner = g[y0 + 1:y1, x0 + 1:x1].copy()\n    emit(inner, \"Strip the frame's border, keeping only the interior cells exactly as they are\")\n    return inner\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"s a few hollow, one‑colour rectangular frames, and the output is the interior of the frame with the largest interior area (train 1: colour‑4 frame, 80 cells, beats colour‑2 with 42; train 2: colour‑8 frame, 42, beats colour‑7 with 9; train 3: only one frame, colour 1)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"I therefore test the bbox and the bbox shrunk by one on each side, and accept the largest candidate whose four sides are completely the frame colour."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Select the hollow rectangular frame with the largest interior and cut it out of the noisy grid, border included"}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"every input is random noise plus a few hollow, one‑colour rectangular frames, and the output is the interior of the frame with the largest interior area (train 1: colour‑4 frame, 80 cells, beats colour‑2 with 42; train 2: colour‑8 frame, 42, beats colour‑7 with 9; train 3: only one frame, colour 1)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The crop of each chosen frame's interior matched the output exactly in all three pairs."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"For the test input there are three frames: colour 3 at rows 2–13 / cols 7–18 (interior 10×10 = 100 cells), colour 8 (interior 36) and colour 7 (interior 12)."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The crop of each chosen frame's interior matched the output exactly in all three pairs."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"ing its border (in train 1 a colour‑3 frame has an extra cell under one corner), so the component's bounding box may be one larger than the real frame."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The construction stages: first isolate the chosen frame (crop with its border), then strip the border to leave the interior, which is the final output."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"545e5221072fbcc9d73ef982a9a1e3ce7a1da802434673cb5416b828c73313dc","task_sha256":"56d42c07aafad11c82414279a